meta {
  name: 17 Retrieve Sample (Undo)
  type: http
  seq: 17
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/accession/order-samples/{{sampleId}}/retrieve
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{businessAccessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "retrievedFrom": "Main Lab",
    "notes": "Recalled the sample",
    "attachmentUrl": ""
  }
}

tests {
  test("transition applied (or rejected by the state machine)", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.be.oneOf([200, 201, 422]);
  });
}

docs {
  Cancelled order: Retrieve is a wind-down and is allowed ONLY for a sample that is out at
  another branch / partner (Sent, Forwarded, Outsourced). Any other sample of a cancelled
  order is refused 422 SAMPLE_ORDER_CANCELLED ("This order has been cancelled, so Retrieve
  is not allowed on this sample ..."), which wins over any other Retrieve refusal. In a
  bulk call with `skipInvalid` that sentence is the skipped `reason`.

  Bulk answer: each entry of `skipped` carries `reason` (readable) and `code` (the same
  error code the strict call answers with, e.g. SAMPLE_RETRIEVE_BLOCKED_RESULT_DONE,
  INVALID_SAMPLE_TRANSITION), so a screen can tell the reasons apart. The HTTP status of a
  group call with `skipInvalid` stays 2xx: the call itself was valid.

  Validated result: a sample that is at this branch is NOT retrieved back to BEFORE its
  acceptance (New / Collected / Halt / Hold / Repeat ...) while any test on it has a
  validated result (Result Done, Approved or Published) - the sample would say "not
  accepted yet" next to a done result. Refused 422 SAMPLE_RETRIEVE_BLOCKED_RESULT_DONE
  (skipped with that reason in a bulk call with `skipInvalid`); use Re-run to redo a
  validated result. An undo that stays at / after acceptance (Stored -> Accepted ...),
  a result that is not validated yet (it stays saved) and a sample that is out at
  another branch (its transfer rules apply) are not affected. Nothing is deleted.

  Retrieve / Retry (§A.7/§A.10.19) — the **universal undo**. Moves the sample back
  to its **immediately-previous status from its own history**: the `fromStatus` of
  the most recent NON-retrieve transition INTO the current status. Repeated
  retrieves therefore walk the real timeline (New → Collected → Accepted ⇒
  Accepted → Collected → New), rather than oscillating on a single stored previous
  status. A sample with **no prior history** (just created, or already back at its
  first status) is a no-op — the transition is "invalid", so a bulk call with
  `skipInvalid` skips it gracefully instead of forcing a status. A transferred
  sample (Sent / Forward / Outsourced / Returned) reverts to **Accepted** via the
  §A.9 send/forward/outsource history row.

  **Cross-branch recall** — pulling a **Forwarded / Outsourced** sample back (Retrieve,
  or Repeat) unwinds the whole transfer so no stale record is left: every open
  transfer is **soft-deleted** (deletedAt) so it leaves the Referral / Outsource
  queues (which filter `deletedAt IS NULL`). The source sample's `processingBranchId`
  is reset to its origin and the dispatch fields cleared. (A partner / outsource
  centre has no branch copy, so there is no clone to remove.)

  **Sent (INTERNAL) samples are different:** the sender can Retrieve only until the
  receiving branch has received the sample (transfer IN_TRANSIT / PICKED_UP). The open
  INTERNAL transfer is then closed as **REJECTED** (reason "Recalled via Retrieve") AND
  **soft-deleted** (`deletedAt`), so it leaves the receiving branch's Internal Transfer
  list, dashboard counts and open-transfer checks; the sample returns to its previous
  status (Accepted). The row is kept in the database, and the recall is recorded in the
  **Audit log** (module ACCESSION, written in the same transaction as the Retrieve, so it
  never exists without it): one entry for the sending branch ("Retrieved sample ACC-... from
  <Branch B> while In-Transit|Picked Up", or "... (no center assigned yet)") and, when the
  receiving branch had been able to see the transfer, one for the receiving branch ("Sample
  ACC-... was retrieved by <Branch A> before <Branch B> received it (it was ...)"), each with
  sample, order, transfer, stage and both branches in `metadata`. The sender's sample history
  row carries the same sentence in its notes. The Audit page is in Business Admin. Once the
  receiver has received or accepted it, Retrieve is refused (422 SAMPLE_RETRIEVE_BLOCKED);
  the receiving branch sends it back with its own Reject / Repeat. The sender cannot
  Repeat a Sent sample (422 SAMPLE_SENT_TO_BRANCH).

  Body (all optional): `retrievedFrom` (station / lab the sample is retrieved from —
  stored on the retrieve history row's `reason`), `notes`, `attachmentUrl`.

  The bulk twin is `POST /accession/order-samples/bulk/retrieve` with `{ "ids": [...],
  "retrievedFrom"?, "notes"?, "attachmentUrl"?, "skipInvalid"? }` — the grouped
  in-house page's "Retrieve All" stepping modal sends each group's `sampleIds` with
  `skipInvalid: true` (samples not in a retrievable state are skipped).

  **Sent – Repeated / Sent – Rejected (sender's tube after the receiving branch sends it back).**
  When the receiving branch Repeats or Rejects an INTERNAL transfer (before or after
  Accept), the SENDER's original tube is NOT moved to Repeat any more: it stays
  `SENT_INTERNAL`, its transfer is closed as `REPEAT` / `REJECTED` (with the receiving
  branch's reason), and the sender's list reads "Sent – Repeated" / "Sent – Rejected"
  (the reason is the tooltip; the sender's history also gets a "returned by the
  receiving branch" row, no status change). The report is already reset and back at the
  sender, view-only until the sender decides. The sender then chooses:
    - **Sent – Repeated:** `POST order-samples/:id/repeat` (tube -> Repeat);
    - **Sent – Rejected:** `POST order-samples/:id/error` (tube -> Error, then Repeat).
  Repeat is refused (422 `SAMPLE_SENT_TO_BRANCH`) on a Sent tube whose transfer is still
  open or was rejected, and Error on one whose transfer is open or was repeated - even for a
  forced group call. Only the SENDING branch may do it (403 `TRANSFER_WRONG_BRANCH`),
  like Retrieve. Retrieve itself is REFUSED on a sent-back tube (422 `SAMPLE_RETRIEVE_BLOCKED`: it would only put the tube back to Accepted as if nothing had happened). The sender's Repeat / Error does NOT delete the receiving branch's
  closed transfer; the referring panel is notified at this point (not when the receiving
  branch clicked). On a cancelled order the tube stays frozen: Repeat, Error and Retrieve are all refused.
}
